WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.569
With over four decades of Star Wars movies,
books and TV shows to pore over, fans have

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.960
uncovered a myriad of mistakes and inconsistencies
in the universe over the years.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.740
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video, I'll be explaining how

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.950
the new Solo film addresses or fixes five
intriguing Star Wars movie mistakes, contradictions

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.260
and unanswered questions.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:25.890
If you're new here, I do regular movie breakdowns,
so subscribe to get all my new Star Wars videos

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.890
and leave a comment as well for a chance to
win a Star Wars Funko Pop!

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:34.300
Spoilers ahead for the Solo movie, so take
care if you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.241
Something that's puzzled die-hard Star Wars
fans for a while is how the Millennium Falcon

00:00:38.241 --> 00:00:41.670
can have a conversation with droids like C3PO
and R2-D2?

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:46.050
"Sir, I don't know where your ship learned
to communicate, but it has the most peculiar

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.050
dialect."

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.690
"I believe, sir, it says that the power coupling
on the negative axis has been polarized."

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:56.820
And the Solo movie finally reveals that it's
Lando's cranky droid L3 who's behind the Millennium

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.630
Falcon's rather quirky personality.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:05.009
After L3 kicks off a droid rebellion on Kessel,
she gets blasted and her system starts failing.

00:01:05.009 --> 00:01:08.690
Lando manages to save part of her and takes
her onto the Millennium Falcon.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:12.240
During their escape, the crew have to take
a shortcut through the Maelstrom which is

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.970
extremely dangerous, but Han points out that
L3 has "the best navigational database in

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:20.860
the galaxy" and suggests downloading her brain
into the Falcon's computer.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.900
L3 then maps the area and finds the best way
out.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.950
So in the words of Lando, she's "a part of
the ship now."

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.440
This also fits something revealed in The Last
Jedi novelisation where it's said that "the

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.290
Falcon has always been cantankerous" and that
one of its brains "had a fondness for both

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:40.451
romantic gossip and dirty jokes" which fits
in well with L3's personality as we saw it

00:01:40.451 --> 00:01:41.950
in the Solo movie.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:46.010
It might also explain why Lando is still protective
of the ship when we meet him in The Empire

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:50.350
Strikes Back as it still houses the consciousness
so to speak of his old friend.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:56.100
By the way, L3's brain module originated as
an R3 astromech brain which also ties in neatly

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.850
with the specs for the Millennium Falcon which
state the ship's computer is made up of 3

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.259
droid brains one of which is an R3 astromech.

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:08.290
When we first meet Han Solo in A New Hope,
he boasts about how the Millennium Falcon

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:11.299
made the Kessel Run in under 12 parsecs.

00:02:11.299 --> 00:02:15.810
Since then though canon stories have thrown
doubt on whether Han's achievement is actually

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:16.810
true.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:20.909
In the canon novel "A New Hope: The Princess,
The Scoundrel, and The Farm Boy", it's revealed

00:02:20.909 --> 00:02:25.939
that when Han makes his boast in the Mos Eisley
cantina, it's actually a lie and that Obi-Wan

00:02:25.939 --> 00:02:27.439
probably knew better.

00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:31.629
And amusingly, by the time of The Force Awakens,
it seems that Rey's heard stories that it

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:33.379
actually took around 14 parsecs.

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:38.139
"This is the ship that made the Kessel Run
in 14 parsecs?"

00:02:38.139 --> 00:02:39.359
"Twelve!"

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:41.799
"(SCOFFS) Fourteen."

00:02:41.799 --> 00:02:45.889
The Solo movie weighs in on the confusion
when, after completing the run, Han boasts

00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:50.299
to the guy handling the fuel cells that he
just did the Kessel Run in 12 parsecs.

00:02:50.299 --> 00:02:54.939
Then Chewie seems to object to this claim,
and Han replies, "not if you round down, buddy".

00:02:54.939 --> 00:02:59.329
Now, exactly how much rounding-down is going
on here isn't revealed, but it sounds like

00:02:59.329 --> 00:03:04.109
those 14 parsecs mentioned in The Force Awakens
might be closer to the truth than fans thought

00:03:04.109 --> 00:03:06.499
when we first saw that scene.

00:03:06.499 --> 00:03:10.529
If you watch or more accurately listen to
a Star Wars movie, you'll start to notice

00:03:10.529 --> 00:03:15.199
that names are often pronounced inconsistently,
something due in part to the mix of American

00:03:15.199 --> 00:03:17.790
and British actors and their differing accents.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.919
And there's one major character in particular
whose name's been said inconsistently throughout

00:03:21.919 --> 00:03:22.919
the movies.

00:03:22.919 --> 00:03:23.919
"Han Solo."

00:03:23.919 --> 00:03:26.019
"Well, take care of yourself Han."

00:03:26.019 --> 00:03:27.019
"What?"

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:28.019
"Han..."

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:29.019
"It sounds like Han."

00:03:29.019 --> 00:03:30.939
"There's still a chance to save Han..."

00:03:30.939 --> 00:03:32.079
"Oh, it's Han."

00:03:32.079 --> 00:03:35.180
"you think that after what you did to Han"
"Han, we need you."

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.220
"nor was giving Han to this bounty hunter!"

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.220
"What about Han?"

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.489
"Han, get up here!"

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:40.489
"Han?!"

00:03:40.489 --> 00:03:42.150
"We're trying to save Han from the bounty
hunter!"

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.989
"Princess, we'll find Han."

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:45.620
"We're short one Han Solo."

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.950
"You're Han Solo?"

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:47.950
"Goodness!

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:48.950
Han Solo!"

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.849
"You are the Han Solo that fought with the
Rebellion."

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:54.099
"Han Solo!"

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:55.349
"Han Solo!"

00:03:55.349 --> 00:03:59.579
In the original trilogy, many American actors
say "Hon", while British actors pronounce

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:05.379
it "Han", Carrie Fisher's Leia switches between
"Hon" and "Han", George Lucas himself says

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:10.430
"Han" and Lando repeatedly pronounces it "Han"
despite Billy Dee Williams being American.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:14.270
The Solo movie decides to acknowledge this
inconsistency, explaining it in the scene

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.730
where Han meets Lando for the first time.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:17.790
"Han Solo."

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.780
"Can I ask you a question Captain Calrissian?"

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.000
"Anything Han."

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.220
"It's Han.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.410
But that's OK."

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.630
Perhaps Lando was just trying to put Han off
his game, but it seems like the pronunciation

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:34.450
stuck and we now have an explanation for the
way Lando says it.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:38.700
Rogue One already gave us a little theory
about why Stormtroopers so often miss their

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:43.470
targets when it comes to Force users, but
the Solo movie takes the explanation a stage

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.950
further and answers the perennial question
of how imperial troops can be so generally

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:52.070
incompetent when shooting even people who
aren't Force sensitive.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:56.620
In a bit of meta-commentary, after removing
a Mudtrooper uniform, Jon Favreau's alien

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.790
character Rio says, "How do they breathe in
these suits?

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:00.870
It's so stuffy.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.910
No wonder they're such lousy pilots.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:06.320
You can't turn your head, you can't breathe,
you can't hear each other."

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.390
Obviously, he's not directly addressing Stormtrooper
incompetence rather Mudtroopers, but it seems

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:15.910
reasonable to assume that Stormtroopers also
suffer from having to wear clunky, stuffy

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:20.510
suits as well, hence the reason for their
infamous inability to shoot accurately.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:25.410
At the end of Solo, the movie tackles the
controversial change that George Lucas made

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:30.640
when he re-edited the original Star Wars for
the 1997 Special Edition re-release.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.010
Lucas altered the scenes in the cantina where
bounty hunter Greedo corners Han about the

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:36.550
money he owes Jabba.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:40.770
Originally, after Han said "over my dead body",
Greedo bragged that he "looking forward to

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.070
this", prompting Han to shoot him dead.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:47.180
George Lucas was unhappy with this portrayal
of Han saying it showed him to be a "cold-blooded

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:52.750
killer", so for the 1997 release he added
frames showing Greedo shooting Han first but

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:53.750
missing.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:58.580
Curiously, the 2004 DVD release edited the
scene again having Greedo and Han shoot at

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.590
each other around the same time.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:05.440
The Solo movie heavily implies that Han most
probably did shoot first in the cantina, because

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:09.580
he does the same to Beckett, shooting him
dead as he's talking and readying his own

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:10.580
gun.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:15.090
Solo sets Han's character up as someone operating
in a morally-grey area, both willing to help

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:20.180
out the good guys like Enfys Nest and also
work for people like Jabba the Hut.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:24.100
And it's Han's transition from rogue to hero
in A New Hope that many fans love about the

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:29.320
character which is why it feels important
that Solo finally establishes that Han did

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:30.500
shoot first.

